lination  and that increase in cell size the cells  become highly evacuated  and we see a decrease in cell wall  thickness we also see an increase in  cell size  in the exocarp cells but in that case it  also includes an increase  in cell wall and middle lamella  thickness  so to perform the qtl analysis this a 45  000  point snip array spanning the cucumber  genome is currently  being developed by ronophir and amir  sherman to allow for qtl  mapping and this is being done based on  the availability of two  sequenced cucumber lines with dramatic  differences in fruit size and shape  the first is chinese long 9930 this was  the first published cucumber genome  sequence  by huang ital from the beijing genomics  institute  and the second was sequenced by equine  wang and all in collaboration with rosh  454  this was a chinese ginishas pickling  cucumber line  wang also his group also made f7 real  populations  crosses derived from gy14 the pickling  type by the chinese lawn  so as a starting point to identify  separable qtl  all that can contribute to these  differences in fruit size and shape  we sought to identify growth parameters  that differ between the two cultivars  so factors that we're looking at  includes ovule number cell number at  anthesis  increase in cell number post anthesis  period of cell division  period of cell expansion cell size cell  shape and preferred plane of cell  division  we can see that differences in ovary  size and shape are as parent as early  between the chinese long and the  pickling type as seven  days pre-anthesis indicating the  importance of early development factors  influencing ovary shape and in this case  ovary length  here there was a significant difference  in over length at  seven days pre thesis and that  difference continued to occur  until we hit anthesis where the chinese  long  were much longer than the pickling type  both of the cucumber fruits exhibited a  typical sigmoidal  growth pattern and as we can expect we  see an increase in cell number  preceding the increase in fruit and cell  size  if we look at length and diameter in  comparison to each other among  segregating f2 progeny  we see that they are not correlated  indicating that the factors deriving  driving increase in fruit length are  separable  from those driving fruit diameter  there is also a difference in overall  number with the chinese long  approximately twice as many as the  pickling type and f1 falling almost  immediately in between if we look for a  correlation between ovule number  and fruit length we see that that is  correlated but there's no correlation  with fruit diameter indicating that  ovule number is a strong determinant of  over a length  but not diameter  if we look at cell number in  longitudinal section we see that at  anthesis the chinese lung already had a  greater number of cells  and post anthesis there continues to be  an increase in cell number for a longer  period cell division is occurring  longer than in the pickling type  cell size however is essentially  equivalent between the two  in contrast if we look at ovary diameter  just like we saw with ovary length  the chinese lung at seven days  pre-enthusiast was lot  bigger than the pickling type but as we  approached in thesis instead of that  difference getting bigger  that difference actually disappeared and  post pollination we see that the  increase in diameter was much  faster for the pickling type than the  chinese long  if we look at increase in cell number we  see that there was a  more rapid and a longer period of cell  division occurring in cross section for  the  pickling type than chinese lung leading  to a three-fold greater cell number  in the pickling type in diameter  the two lines also exhibited differences  in cell shape gy14  cell size was pretty much equivalent  whether you're looking at longitudinal  cross-sections  whereas there was a difference in the  dimensions for the  chinese law so collectively these  results indicate that there are several  factors pre  and post anthesis influencing fruit size  and shape the number of ovules  the number of cells in longitudinal  section or cross-section  the length of period of cell division  and either fruit elongation or fruit  with expansion  post-pollination and cell shape  so these observations suggest that there  are distinct traits that we can now  analyze in the real  population for future qtl analysis  and finally i'd like to talk about our  work studying transcriptome  analysis of early fruit development and  for this a set of three fruit growth  analysis experiments was performed in  the greenhouse  we wanted to be able to monitor changes  in gene expression  and relate those to obvious stages in  fruit development  one of the things is that typically  people look at that initial stage it's  not very interesting  you know the fruit set and then they  kind of grow and then they do the  exciting stuff at the end if they're  going to ripen they make flavors and  aromas and colors  but when we started to look carefully at  what's happening during that early stage  of growth in cucumbers  there actually is a lot happening  there's a highly reproducible  progression  of changes and features including a loss  of ridges  the formation and then subsidence  of warts the development and then  incision  of spines changes in wax coverage and  chlorophyll content and patterns and  placenta and seed development  another interesting feature during early  cucumber  fruit development is important with  respect to infection by the important oh  my seat pathogen phytophthora capsacy  from some work that we had been done to  try to screen for  cucumber fruits that were resistant to  this pathogen  for breeding purposes at that time we  were not  successful in identifying a source of  resistance for breeding  however we did discover that there was a  developmental  progression in resistance or an  age-related resistance to this disease  such that young fruit are very highly  susceptible and they become resistant  as they develop and this transition from  susceptible to resistance  was directly correlated with that  transition away from the period  of rapid fruit elongation  so collectively these observations show  that there's a lot of changes occurring  during early fruit development and they  provide a physiological context  from which to examine changes in gene  expression  so to perform the transcriptomic  analysis fruits were sampled at 0  4 8 12 and 16 days post pollination  this allowed us to cover that  exponential period of growth  and also include the time at which  cucumber fruits would typically  be harvested rna and cdna libraries were  prepared for samples that included  pericarp and placenta but did not  include seeds  454 pyro sequencing provided  approximately 1.1 million ests or  approximately 200  000 clean reads per time point and  approximately 82  of those could be assembled into a set  of about 28 000 contigs  the mean contig length was 834 and the  mean number of reads per content was 67.  now one of the first things we wanted to  do was look at the relationship between  the number of reads  and our ability to assemble context of  course you would imagine as you have  more reads you can put together a longer  context  but at some point you're going to reach  the full transcript length and it's not  going to get any longer  so if we look at that we see a steady  increase in the average contact length  as our number of reads increases  but it then leveled off at about 1400  base pairs that's a typical transcript  length that's what we would expect  and that occurred at approximately 30  ests per contig  we also asked what was the relationship  between the number of reads and our  ability  to identify putative homologues in the  databases  and we could see with arabidopsis that  it also steadily increased  and then leveled off at about 30 reads  per contig  and so we then used 30 ests per contig  as a baseline  for our subsequent bioinformatic  analysis  now at the at the point where we leveled  off  approximately 90 percent of our contigs  had putative homologs in arabidopsis  and if we look at the putative  functional assignments of those  genes we see that they fall into a  distribution pretty similar to what you  would see in arabidopsis within about  two-fold  range for the or the categories in  rabidoxis suggesting good representation  of the genome but there were 10 percent  that did not have putative homologues in  arabidopsis  now approximately five percent  approximately half of those did have  putative homologues in other speeches  such as grape and popular for which  their extensive  gene bank entries but the final five  percent either did not have identified  homologues  in the current database or they only had  homologues in other  cucurbit species suggesting that these  may be  unique to cucumber or other cu turbines  relative to the kinds of plant species  that have been sequenced today  if we then looked at the distribution of  these  transcripts that did not have putative  homologues  we see that that was not equivalent at  the different time points  so there were approximately twice as  many that did not have  putative homologs in arabidopsis at 8 12  and 16 days post pollination  as at zero and four days suggesting that  that post fruit set that exponential  growth phase  may include more cucurbit novel genes  if we look at those transcripts  represented by greater than 30 ests  but that had homologues only identified  in cucurbit species  that included about 18 that had putative  functional assignments  and of those eight were related to  phloem  such as slow filament protein phloem  lectin etc and this is consistent with  the unique  phloem structure in cucurbits relative  to other species  we then asked wanted to look at the  relationship  between the genes expressed at the  different ages using principal component  analysis  and this cl put the different ages into  three groups  zero and four days post pollination  eight days post pollination  and twelve and sixteen days post  pollination  can then superimpose that on the growth  curve  and we see that zero and four is that  early fruit set cell division phase  eight peak exponential growth 12 and 16  late or post exponential growth  we next want to be able to do  comparisons between gene expression at  the different ages and this is based on  the assumption  that the number of reads that we're  seeing is actually reflective of the  transcript level  and we were comforted  in two ways with respect to that  question one was that if we looked at  samples  um from eight days post pollination two  sequencing runs we see a very good  correlation  between those suggesting that those that  have low level expression indeed have  low expression  those that have high have high  expression and then we went on to select  a subset of approximately 20 genes that  had differing levels of expression at  each of the time points  and for those we did qrt pcr analysis  we saw good correspondence between the  454 data  and the qrt pcr data and that qrt pcr  analysis was actually performed on two  sets of experiments that had been  done in the greenhouse at different  times so we saw a good correspondence  between the two  sets of qrt indicating good biological  confirmation or reproducibility as well  we then looked at which of those  constructs or sorry which of the  transcripts were most  highly represented specifically at one  age not ones that are highly represented  all the way across  but are there ones that are uniquely  high what's blasting at zero or four  or at eight so if we look at zero and  four we see that there's a  those that are blasting include histones  and ribosome associated proteins  that makes sense these cells are  undergoing cell division they need to  have dna replication they need to have  lots of protein synthesis  histones pretty much disappear by the  time you get to eight days there's a  little carryover  on the ribosomal but what's most  striking however  is that we look at eight days post  pollination we see that more than half  52 of those genes either had unknown  function  or did not have either did not have  homologs in rabidoxis  or if they did we don't know what they  do so this large  proportion of contents without homologs  or with unknown function  may reflect either cucurbit unique genes  or less extensive characteristic  characterization of the exponential  growth phase  if we then look at that group that was  very highly expressed at eight days post  pollination  and we compare that to predicted  arabidopsis homologs  what we see is very different from that  earlier slide i just showed you  this is where everything else fell  within a two-fold range  but here we see that there are some  groups that are very highly  over-represented  and these were typically associated with  cell structure  so for example we see very high level of  expression of aquaporins  be necessary for water uptake to derive  cell expansion  other examples would be the phloem  related proteins which would be  necessary for import of photoassimilate  as well as some of the structural  proteins such as actins or alpha  tubulins  some of the other categories that were  highly expressed were genes associated  with cell walls  again you would expect that for growth  as well as several hormone-associated  genes including  a key enzyme and brassinosteroid  biosynthesis  and brass and steroids have been shown  to be critical for cucumber fruit set in  early development  and they can induce cucumber fruit  growth  another category that was very highly  expressed was latex  associated proteins and lipid associated  proteins  well that was looking at things that are  just blasting what's  really highly expressed at each of these  time points but we're also interested in  genes that may not be expressed  that strongly but still show a very  specific time point of expression  so in order to get a look at that what  we did was we said  let's normalize according to the  proportion of expression  so if a gene was expressed at the same  level throughout this period of  development there are five time points  and you'd see  twenty percent of expression at each of  those ages  so now for example let's look at eight  days post pollination  let's take all of the genes and say what  percentage  of their transcripts occurred at eight  days  and what we see is a very strong  curve with a peak almost exactly at  twenty percent  indicating that the majority of genes  had twenty percent of their expression  at that time point  however we can also see that there's a  long tail  similarly if we look at zero plus four  now you've got two time points together  you'd expect forty percent  or twelve percent plus sixteen and again  they fell at exactly  forty percent so then we asked  what's happening though in these tails  the top  2.5 of genes  can we identify transcripts that were  are specifically enriched  and so what we get here is three  non-overlapping sets of transcripts  0 and 4 8 or 12 and 16  and not only are they not overlapping  with each other but in large part  it was a new set of genes because  earlier we were looking at the most  highly expressed  they had to be at least 0.1 percent on  average they would have been  1700 to 2000 reads per transcript  now we're looking at ones that are on  average 100 to 200  reads per transcript  when we perform functional analysis we  see different kinds of  genes showing enhancement at the  different age groups  so for example at zero and four days we  see an increase as we saw  earlier with cell organogenesis  biogenesis dna and rna metabolism  and here's an example here what we're  looking at is several putative cell  division related genes cyclin and  cyclin-dependent kinases  we see very high expression at zero and  four and then essentially  no expression after that  another example of something that was  uniquely expressed at a specific time  point are several lipid associated  proteins  gl sf motate lipases  lipid transfer proteins and this  actually corresponds  with the time period in which we're  seeing both increase in cuticle  thickness and change in cuticle  structure  if we looked at those that are more  enriched at 12 and 16 days post  pollination  we say that these had the highest  represented representation of abiotic  and biotic stress-related genes  including a variety of heat shock redox  ethylene related transcripts  suggesting a change in emphasis from  growth and defense  from growth to defense  so in conclusion the contig set suggests  broad representation of the cucumber  transcriptome  approximately five percent of the highly  expressed transcripts did not have a  match  or only matched cucurbit clones  suggesting that there may be genes  unique to cucurbits principal component  analysis  placed the ages in three groups zero and  four  days plus pollination characterized by  cell division  and transcripts most highly expressed  included genes associated with cell  division  organogenesis and biogenesis at eight  days post pollination we're at  peak exponential growth highly enriched  for transcripts associated with cell  structure  growth lipid metabolism and defense  and also highly very highly expressed  genes that did not have homologs or  unknown function and finally at 12 and  16 days post pollination the late or  post-exponential growth  exhibited increased expression of  stress-related and environmental  response and extracellular associated  genes  so collectively these patterns of  expression are consistent with a profile  of early  rapid growth followed by an emphasis on  defense  and with that i want to make sure to  acknowledge the people who actually do  the work  graduate student the postdoc calorie  andy and mariah mccauley worked on fruit  development  sex expression katarina papadopoulos  holly little and jessica taft  and the great majority of our melon  transformation is done by our technician  sue hammer as well as thank you to the  agencies that have supported the work  thank you  yeah i was curious in the end uh  you didn't identify any transformation  oh actually there  yeah there are lots of transcription  factors in there they obviously  um don't show up in the most highly  expressed category but we've got lots  and lots of transcription factors  there's so many intricate developmental  things going on  yeah yeah yeah we do have lots  but they're not we were at this point  focusing on the more highly expressed  kinds of genes judy going back to your  sex expression work um the acs  gene has anyone looked at diversity  within that gene across  the german positive to see if there's  evidence for selection  because i  you're talking about the enzymatic  activity or  expression or  just raw nucleotide diversity um  what has been done so far not by us so  for example  the there was a french group that cloned  the different acs genes  and the one from melon what they had  done was  looking at different sex types and then  pulling out  extremes so for example the acs-7 gene  in a plant that's capable of producing  female flowers is functional  but the acs-7 gene in this  one we work with which is bisexual it  still produces that gene we can detect  with  qrt we can detect expression of that  gene but it's a non-functional enzyme  it has a mutation in an active site  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu


